"Written to `sync_workspace/sync_rules.md` 📝\n\nThe core rule (**R1**): when field `K` is deleted in one file, the peer file gets `\"K\": { \"__tombstone__\": true }` instead of silently dropping the key.\n\nI also pinned down the edge cases that usually bite later, so the rule is actually implementable:\n\n- **Tombstones aren't live data** — readers must treat them as deleted fields\n- **Idempotent** — re-applying the same deletion is a no-op\n- **Re-creation wins** — assigning a real value clears the tombstone in the peer\n- **Update beats delete** — if one side modifies while the other deletes in the same sync window, the modification wins\n- **Optional GC** — tombstones can be purged only after both files agree the key is dead\n\nWant me to implement the sync engine next (e.g. a Python script that applies R1 to `source.json`/`target.json`)?"